{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Moses took the tent.</b> Rashi writes (Rashi on Exodus 33:11) that this practice of Moses continued from Yom HaKippurim until the Tabernacle was erected, no longer — for on the 17th of Tammuz the tablets were broken, on the 18th he burned the calf and judged the sinners, on the 19th he went up and spent forty days there, on the first of Elul it was said to him (Exodus 34:2), \"And you shall go up in the morning,\" to receive the second tablets, and he spent another forty days there, on the 10th of Tishrei HaKippurim, the Holy One, blessed be He, was reconciled to Israel and delivered to him the last tablets and he came down, and he began to command concerning the work of the Tabernacle, and they made it on the first of Nisan, and from the time the Tabernacle was erected, He did not speak with him except from there. And such is also the opinion of Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra], that all this was after he brought down the second tablets, and there is no earlier and later in the Torah. But this is not correct in my eyes, for what reason would there be to mention this here in the middle of the portion? And the words of our Sages in all the midrashim are that Moses did this on account of the sin of the calf: Moses reasoned — one excommunicated by the master is excommunicated by the disciple — as Rashi mentioned (Rashi on Exodus 33:7), \"I am in anger and you are in anger, so who will bring them near?\" And if the removal of the tent had been after Yom HaKippurim, then both the Holy One, blessed be He, and Moses were in a state of favor. But what appears correct is that on the day of his descent, the 17th of Tammuz, he burned the calf and judged those who had worshipped it, and on the next day he said to them that he would go up to YHWH to atone for them, and he went up to the mountain where the Glory was, and this is \"And Moses returned to YHWH\" (Exodus 32:31), and he prayed a brief prayer, \"Ah, this people has sinned,\" and HaShem answered him, \"Whoever has sinned against Me,\" \"And now go, lead the people,\" and the plague began among them (Exodus 33:33–35), and He commanded him, \"Go, ascend from here, you and the people\" (Exodus 33:1–3), and he reported this to Israel, and they mourned and stripped themselves of their ornaments (Exodus 33:4–6). Then Moses saw that the matter was very prolonged and he did not know what its end would be. And he took the tent and pitched it for himself outside the camp (Exodus 33:7), so that the Shekhinah would speak to him from there, for it does not dwell among the people, and if the tent were within the camp, he would not have communication from there. And it says (Exodus 33:7), <b>and it came to pass that everyone who sought YHWH</b> — the reason: so that everyone who sought YHWH would go out to it. And Scripture proceeds to recount (Exodus 33:8–11) everything that took place while the tent was there until the Tabernacle was erected, which is from Yom HaKippurim until the first of Nisan according to the opinion of our Sages (Shabbat 87a). And I saw in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer (chapter 46): Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korḥah says — forty days Moses spent on the mountain, reciting the scriptural law by day and reviewing the Mishnah law by night, and after forty days he took the tablets and came down to the camp. On the 17th of Tammuz he broke the tablets and killed the sinners of Israel, and he spent forty days in the camp until he burned the calf and ground it to the dust of the earth and eradicated idolatry from Israel and established every tribe in its place. And on the first of Elul the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, \"Ascend the mountain to Me,\" and they sounded the shofar in the camp — for Moses had gone up the mountain — so they would not stray again after idolatry, and the Holy One, blessed be He, was exalted with that shofar, as it is said (Psalms 47:6), \"Elohim has ascended with a teru'ah, YHWH with the sound of the shofar.\" And so the Sages enacted that they should sound the shofar on the first of Elul every single year. Thus far the words of this aggadah. If so, \"And Moses took the tent\" and the entire portion (Exodus 33:7–11) would be from the 18th of Tammuz until the completion of the forty days, and from Yom HaKippurim until the first of Nisan. But this does not fit properly with what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 9:18–19), \"And I fell down before YHWH as at the first, forty days and forty nights; bread I did not eat,\" and so forth, and it is written (Deuteronomy 9:25), \"And I fell down before YHWH the forty days and the forty nights that I fell down, for YHWH said to destroy you,\" and it is impossible that all this was during the last forty days, after it was said to him (Exodus 34:1), \"Carve for yourself two tablets of stone and ascend the mountain to Me,\" for they were in a state of favor, and He had already retracted from destroying you."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The acronym יוה\"כ (Yom HaKippurim) is rendered as Yom HaKippurim throughout. The phrase 'מְנֻדֶּה לָרַב מְנֻדֶּה לַתַּלְמִיד' is rendered literally as a quoted principle; Ramban leaves the reasoning elliptical and so does the translation. The final 'וּכְבָר בִּטֵּל לְהַשְׁמִיד אֶתְכֶם' shifts to second person quoting Deuteronomy's address; rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:21:31.638760800Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}